9701: snapshot records named in Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}.  The dated
9702: venue/bibliography check, when retained, reads external route and locator pages
9703: and writes a dated log with the explicit boundary that it is not acceptance,
9704: upload, archive minting, Lean rebuild, daemon execution, or artifact rerun
9705: evidence.  The final digest pass records the command, source state, environment,
9706: exit code, generated manifest, and log path in
9707: \path{review_bundle/final_digest_generation_run.log}, and computes the SHA--256
9708: table from the already-existing support inputs.  The table deliberately excludes
9709: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} and
9710: \path{review_bundle/final_digest_generation_run.log} as hashed inputs, avoiding a
9711: self-digest recursion; those two files are instead the manifest and the command
9712: metadata for producing it.
9713: 
9714: For the forward implication, the ordering makes every load-bearing finite record
9715: that supports the theorem-ledger coordinate an already-existing byte string before
9716: the digest table is computed.  Hence the digest rows name the same inventory,
9717: extractor-log, verifier-log, dated-venue-log, and selected bundle bytes that are cited in
9718: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-refreshed-coordinate-instantiation}.  By
9719: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-support-surface-closure}, exact finite support is precisely
9720: equality of the selected paths and their final digest rows.  The digest-generator
9721: row in Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory} supplies the command-result
9722: ledger required for the final hash pass itself without requiring the log to be a
9723: hashed input to its own output, so the first coordinate of
9724: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} is closed under the
9725: post-inventory verifier/digest pass without borrowing a pre-inventory or
9726: unlogged command result.
9727: 
9728: For the converse, suppose a step is omitted or run after digest regeneration.  If
9729: the inventory files are missing or stale, the extractor and inventory coverage
9730: claim has no post-inventory input.  If the extractor or verifier logs are missing
9731: or stale, the package has no command-result record with command, source state,
9732: environment, exit code, and log path for the relevant finite comparison.  If a
9733: dated venue-readiness claim is retained but the dated locator check is missing or
9734: stale, the venue statement has no current dated-readiness coordinate.  If the
9735: digest manifest is produced before any of these inputs exists or after they later
9736: change, at least one cited byte string is not the byte string selected by its
9737: final SHA--256 row.  In every case the stronger exact-inclusion coordinate would
9738: require adding a finite record not selected by the manifest, which is forbidden by
9739: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  The fallbacks are
9740: exactly the local/path-verified and dated-readiness readings stated in
9741: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.
9742: 
9743: Finally, none of the five steps contains the finite fields required for the other
9744: evidence axes.  Upload and archive support require the records of
9745: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}; upload-time venue
9746: compliance requires the tuple of
9747: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement}; and fresh
9748: formal-source, daemon, and artifact-semantic claims require the command/source,
9749: environment, exit-code, and log coordinates isolated in
9750: Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}.  Since those coordinates
9751: are not created by the post-inventory fixed-point pass, no such stronger claim is
9752: proved.
9753: \end{proof}
9754: 
9755: \begin{corollary}[Current post-inventory realization]
9756: \label{cor:current-post-inventory-realization}
9757: Assume that the present source state contains the two submitted
9758: inventory files \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}.
9759: If, after those two files exist, the theorem-environment extractor, the retained
9760: finite-record verifiers, the retained dated venue/bibliography check, and the
9761: final SHA--256 manifest are run in the order displayed in
9762: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}, then the theorem-ledger
9763: row $a_7$ of Table~\ref{tab:current-acceptance-ledger} is instantiated at exactly
9764: the finite support level of
9765: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.  In particular the
9766: instantiated row certifies only syntactic inventory coverage, finite-record
9767: schema acceptance, retained dated-readiness logging, and byte-level digest
9768: closure; it does not certify theorem semantics, upload, archive replacement,
9769: venue acceptance, fresh source-theory rebuilds, or dynamic artifact validation.
9770: \end{corollary}
9771: 
9772: \begin{proof}
9773: The first hypothesis supplies the missing finite coordinates selected in
9774: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-package-shape-convention} and in the added row
9775: $a_7$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:nested-package-states}.  The ordered post-write command
9776: sequence is precisely the sequence required in
9777: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  Applying that theorem
9778: therefore makes the resulting digest table a fixed point over the inventory
9779: bytes and the verifier/log bytes that are used by the theorem-ledger claim.
9780: 
9781: It remains to identify the strength of the instantiated support row.  The
9782: normal-form theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form},
9783: classifies the post-inventory coordinate as a finite local support coordinate:
9784: inventory coverage is syntactic, verifier acceptance is schema-relative, dated
9785: venue evidence is local-readiness evidence only, and digest closure is a
9786: byte-string equality surface.  The exclusions listed in the last sentence are
9787: exactly the non-escalation exclusions in that theorem and in
9788: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  Hence the current
9789: inventory row is realized, but only at the stated finite support level.
9790: \end{proof}
9791: 
9792: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record current inventory support-coordinate realization]
9793: \label{thm:current-inventory-artifact-realization}
9794: For the present Stage--A source state, after the final theorem-inventory bytes
9795: for this manuscript have been accepted, suppose that the files
9796: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} are present as
9797: finite package coordinates.  Let $L$ be the retained finite-record log family
9798: consisting of the syntactic theorem-environment extraction log
9799: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log}, the finite
9800: certificate verifier log \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
9801: the source-interface verifier log
9802: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log}, the primary-claim
9803: inventory freshness log
9804: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log}, and any
9805: retained dated venue/bibliography check log used only at its stated historical or
9806: local-readiness level.  If every log in $L$ that is cited as a current
9807: command-result coordinate is regenerated after the two inventory files exist and
9808: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is regenerated after those log bytes
9809: exist, then the two missing support-coordinate obligations labelled
9810: \path{post-inventory-artifact-realization} and
9811: \path{fresh-post-inventory-verifier-and-digest-logs} are discharged exactly at
9812: the support level of Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}: the
9813: inventory files are selected finite coordinates, the extractor and finite-record
9814: verifiers are post-inventory command records, and the digest table names the
9815: resulting inventory and log bytes.  Conversely, if either root inventory file is
9816: absent when a cited current extractor or finite-record verifier log is produced,
9817: or if the final digest table is not regenerated after those cited log bytes
9818: exist, the same support claim is stale for the current inventory bytes and is
9819: rejected as a current fixed-point coordinate.
9820: This realization is only a finite byte/log/digest realization and does not assert extractor semantic completeness, theorem-proof
9821: correctness, venue acceptance, upload, archive replacement, fresh Lean/BEDC
9822: rebuilds, daemon execution, or dynamic artifact validation.
9823: If the inventory files are changed after the extractor or digest pass, the same
9824: statement is no longer current; it reverts to the conditional/historical reading
9825: until the displayed rerun sequence is repeated against the changed bytes.
9826: In the source state used for this Stage--A2 round, the displayed rerun sequence
9827: is instantiated by exit-code-zero records at
9828: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log},
9829: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
9830: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log},
9831: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log}, and
9832: \path{review_bundle/final_digest_generation_run.log}, with the regenerated
9833: manifest at \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}.  This current-instance
9834: sentence is a finite command-result claim only and inherits the same
9835: non-escalation boundary as the theorem.
9836: \end{theorem}
9837: 
9838: \begin{proof}
9839: The first hypothesis supplies the two finite byte coordinates that were absent
9840: from the pre-run support surface.  The theorem-environment extractor then reads
9841: \path{main.tex} together with \path{theorem_inventory.json}; by
9842: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary}, an
9843: exit-code-zero refreshed log proves precisely syntactic label coverage in the
9844: five-environment domain and proves no semantic theorem property.  The certificate
9845: and source-interface verifiers are finite programs of the kind isolated in
9846: Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness} and
9847: Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}.  Rerunning each cited
9848: current verifier after the inventory bytes exist is therefore exactly the
9849: finite-order condition that makes its command, source-state, environment,
9850: exit-code, and log-path record a post-inventory record rather than a stale
9851: pre-inventory record.  The retained dated venue/bibliography check, if used,
9852: contributes only the dated readiness coordinate classified in
9853: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.
9854: 
9855: Finally, regenerating \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} after those
9856: files and logs exist makes the digest table a deterministic SHA--256 function of
9857: the same finite byte strings used by the inventory and verifier claims.  This is
9858: exactly the ordered fixed-point construction of
9859: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}, so the two named issue
9860: obligations are realized as local finite-record support coordinates.  The
9861: current-instance sentence is obtained by reading the named post-inventory logs:
9862: each finite verifier log has exit code zero, and the digest-generation log records
9863: exit code zero for regenerating the final SHA--256 manifest after those logs
9864: exist.  The negative part follows from the same fixed-point theorem and from
9865: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}: none of the listed
9866: finite rows contains the fields required for semantic proof checking, external
9867: publication, source-theory rebuilds, daemon runs, or artifact-semantic reruns.
9868: If an inventory byte changes later, the extractor log and final digest table no
9869: longer name the changed byte string.  The fixed-point theorem then applies only
9870: to the earlier byte tuple, so the current claim is recovered only by rerunning
9871: the sequence and regenerating the digest rows.
9872: 
9873: The converse is the same finite-coordinate argument read backwards.  If either
9874: inventory file was absent when a cited current extractor or verifier log was
9875: produced, then that log has no finite input coordinate equal to the present
9876: inventory byte string.  If the digest table predates one of the cited log bytes,
9877: then the digest table cannot select that log byte as part of the same tuple.  In
9878: either case the candidate support surface lacks one of the displayed finite
9879: coordinates.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} supplies
9880: that missing coordinate as the bad subrecord, so a pre-inventory or pre-digest
9881: record cannot discharge the current fixed-point obligation.
9882: \end{proof}
9883: 
9884: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record current command-result closure]
9885: \label{thm:current-command-result-closure}
9886: Assume the current source package contains the accepted inventory files
9887: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}.  After those
9888: bytes exist, suppose that every command-result sentence retained for the
9889: submission state cites one of the command-boundary rows in
9890: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}, that the corresponding command log is
9891: rerun from the source state named in that log with exit code zero, and that
9892: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is regenerated after all such logs
9893: exist.  Then the command-result claims of the submission state are closed exactly
9894: at their recorded finite level: each accepted claim has command, source state,
9895: environment, exit code, and log path, and every stronger reading not represented
9896: by such a row remains only historical, conditional, path-verified, or absent as
9897: specified by the row boundary.  In particular, the closed command-result surface
9898: does not imply external upload, archive replacement, venue acceptance, Lean/BEDC
9899: rebuild success, daemon execution, theorem-proof semantics, or dynamic artifact
9900: validation.
9901: \end{theorem}
9902: 
9903: \begin{proof}
9904: By Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}, the command-result
9905: sentences used by the current package are exhausted by the rows of
9906: Table~\ref{tab:command-boundary-inventory}.  Each such row is a finite record
9907: whose positive coordinates are the command, the named source state, the
9908: environment, the exit code, and the log path, while its negative boundary states
9909: which stronger assertions are not supplied.  The hypothesis that the retained
9910: logs are rerun after the accepted inventory bytes exist makes those rows
9911: post-inventory command records rather than pre-inventory or stale prose records.
9912: 
9913: The final digest regeneration adds the remaining byte coordinate.  By
9914: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}, the inventory files, the
9915: rerun command logs, and the regenerated digest manifest form the finite local
9916: support fixed point for the edited package.  Therefore every accepted
9917: command-result claim factors through an explicit row containing the five command
9918: metadata fields required by Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.
9919: If a proposed reading asserts upload, archival replacement, venue acceptance,
9920: source-theory rebuild success, daemon execution, theorem semantics, or dynamic
9921: artifact validation, then it changes the evidence level or adds a semantic
9922: coordinate not present in the row.  The finite-coordinate determination theorem,
9923: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, then identifies the
9924: missing coordinate as a finite bad subrecord, so the stronger reading is not
9925: accepted.  The only surviving readings are precisely the historical,
9926: conditional, path-verified, or finite command-result readings declared in the
9927: row boundaries.
9928: \end{proof}
9929: 
9930: \begin{corollary}[Current post-inventory support tuple instantiation]
9931: \label{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}
9932: In the current source package, after \path{theorem_inventory.json} and
9933: \path{theorem_inventory.md} have been written, the ordered tuple consisting of \path{theorem_inventory.json},
9934: \path{theorem_inventory.md},
9935: \path{review_bundle/theorem_environment_extraction_run.log},
9936: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log},
9937: \path{review_bundle/source_interface_verification_run.log},
9938: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory_freshness_2026-06-09.log}, and
9939: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} instantiates the first coordinate of
9940: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} exactly when the
9941: extractor and finite-record verifiers are rerun after the inventory files exist
9942: and the final digest table is regenerated after those logs exist.  The resulting
9943: coordinate is a finite local support tuple only; it is not theorem semantics,
9944: venue submission, archive replacement, Lean/BEDC rebuild evidence, daemon
9945: evidence, or dynamic artifact-validation evidence.
9946: \end{corollary}
9947: 
9948: \begin{proof}
9949: The two inventory files are the finite byte coordinates whose absence blocks the
9950: post-inventory fixed-point claim.  Once they exist, the theorem-environment
9951: extractor reads \path{main.tex} and \path{theorem_inventory.json}, and the
9952: certificate and source-interface verifiers read the finite records specified in
9953: their scripts and in Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}.
9954: Running these commands after the inventory write therefore produces command-log
9955: bytes whose input side includes the inventory state rather than a pre-inventory
9956: state.
9957: 
9958: If \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is then regenerated after those
9959: logs exist, each support input displayed before the digest manifest is selected
9960: by a post-inventory byte row, and the digest manifest itself is the generated
9961: finite selector for those rows.  Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} says precisely
9962: that this ordered acyclic construction closes the first coordinate of the normal form in
9963: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}, and
9964: Proposition~\ref{prop:final-refreshed-coordinate-instantiation} identifies the
9965: same paths as the refreshed finite support tuple.  Hence the tuple instantiates
9966: the current theorem-ledger support coordinate.
9967: 
9968: Conversely, if the inventory write, either verifier rerun, or digest
9969: regeneration is missing or out of order, at least one tuple component is not a
9970: post-inventory byte selected by the final digest manifest.  The exact-inclusion
9971: coordinate would then require an unstated finite row, which is excluded by
9972: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}.  The final sentence
9973: is the non-escalation boundary already proved in
9974: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} and
9975: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.
9976: \end{proof}
9977: 
9978: \begin{corollary}[Byte-level stable replay criterion for the post-inventory tuple]
9979: \label{cor:stable-replay-post-inventory-tuple}
9980: Let $S$ be a source state of the present presentation package in which
9981: the final accepted \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}
9982: exist.  Suppose
9983: that the theorem-environment extractor, certificate-record verifier,
9984: source-interface verifier, and retained primary-claim verifier are run from $S$,
9985: that their logs have exit code zero, and that
9986: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is regenerated after those logs are
9987: written.  Then every later support claim for the first coordinate of
9988: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} factors through the
9989: finite tuple of Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}:
9990: the claim is valid for $S$ exactly as a byte-level replay of that tuple, and any
9991: stronger claim must add a new command-result coordinate of the kind listed in
9992: Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}.  If either inventory file
9993: is later rewritten, this replay criterion applies only historically to the old
9994: tuple until the extractor/verifier/digest sequence is run again from the new
9995: source state.
9996: \end{corollary}
9997: 
9998: \begin{proof}
9999: The hypotheses reproduce the ordered construction required by
10000: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  Since the inventory files
10001: exist before the four retained commands are run, the resulting logs are
10002: post-inventory command records.  Since the digest table is regenerated after the
10003: logs are written, the same finite byte strings are also selected by the final
10004: SHA--256 surface.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}
10005: therefore identifies exactly the support coordinate available in $S$.
10006: 
10007: Now consider any later claim that cites the first coordinate of
10008: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} without adding new
10009: records.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a local
10010: support claim is determined only by its displayed finite coordinates.  The only
10011: displayed coordinates under the hypotheses are the two inventory files, the four
10012: post-inventory logs, and the regenerated digest table that selects those inputs.
10013: Hence the later claim
10014: can use no support surface beyond the tuple just identified.  To assert theorem
10015: semantics, upload, archive replacement, fresh Lean/BEDC rebuilding, daemon
10016: execution, or dynamic artifact validation would require additional command,
10017: source, environment, exit-code, and log data; those are precisely the missing
10018: coordinates isolated in Proposition~\ref{audit:command-run-boundary-inventory}.
10019: Thus the replay criterion is stable and non-escalating.
10020: A later inventory rewrite changes one of the support-input byte strings in the tuple.  Since
10021: the regenerated digest table selects hashes of the old support inputs, the later
10022: state has a different finite coordinate.  The criterion is
10023: therefore stable under byte-level replay of the recorded tuple, but it is not an
10024: automatic certificate for a changed inventory state.
10025: \end{proof}
10026: 
10027: \begin{proposition}[Post-inventory freshness obstruction]
10028: \label{prop:post-inventory-freshness-obstruction}
10029: Let $S$ and $S'$ be two source states of the present package which differ in at
10030: least one byte of \path{theorem_inventory.json} or \path{theorem_inventory.md}.
10031: Suppose the theorem-environment extractor, certificate-record verifier,
10032: source-interface verifier, retained primary-claim verifier, and final digest
10033: generator were run from $S$ in the order required by
10034: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  Then those logs and digest
10035: rows do not instantiate the theorem-inventory coordinate of $S'$.  The coordinate
10036: for $S'$ is available exactly after the same extractor/verifier/digest sequence is
10037: rerun from $S'$ and the digest manifest is regenerated after the new log bytes
10038: exist.  Consequently the two Stage--A issue obligations consisting of the root
10039: theorem-obligation ledger and the fresh verifier/digest support tuple cannot be
10040: closed by a pre-inventory or stale-inventory command record.
10041: \end{proposition}
10042: 
10043: \begin{proof}
10044: The first coordinate of
10045: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} is a finite byte
10046: tuple, not an isomorphism class of prose descriptions.  By
10047: Corollary~\ref{cor:stable-replay-post-inventory-tuple}, a support claim based on
10048: the post-inventory pass factors through the exact byte strings of the two
10049: inventory files, the four retained command logs, and the digest table that was
10050: generated after those logs.  If $S$ and $S'$ differ in either inventory file, then
10051: the input byte tuple for $S'$ is not the input tuple selected by the digest rows
10052: computed from $S$.  Equality of the finite support coordinate therefore fails.
10053: 
10054: The ordered construction in
10055: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} is also sufficient.  Rerunning
10056: the extractor and the three finite-record verifiers from $S'$ makes their command
10057: records post-inventory records for the $S'$ byte strings; regenerating
10058: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} after those logs exist selects the
10059: new inventory and log bytes in the same finite tuple.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}
10060: then identifies that tuple as the current theorem-inventory coordinate, while
10061: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} rejects any stronger
10062: reading that omits one of these finite coordinates.  Thus stale logs prove only a
10063: historical coordinate for $S$, and the current issue obligations close only by the
10064: fresh sequence just described.
10065: \end{proof}
10066: 
10067: \begin{proposition}[Three-coordinate submission-support closure]
10068: \label{prop:three-coordinate-submission-support-closure}
10069: In the present presentation-only package, the three Stage--A support obligations
10070: consisting of the post-inventory theorem-obligation ledger, the dated
10071: venue/bibliography verification record, and the external support surface admit
10072: the following exact normal form.  The theorem-obligation coordinate is closed
10073: precisely by the ordered tuple of
10074: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}: the two
10075: root inventory files exist, the theorem-environment extractor and retained
10076: finite-record verifiers are rerun after those files exist, and the final digest
10077: manifest is regenerated after the resulting log bytes exist.  The
10078: venue/bibliography coordinate is either a dated local-readiness record with
10079: command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path, or else all
10080: venue-readiness language remains conditional or historical.  The external
10081: support coordinate is either exact supplemental upload of the selected support
10082: surface with matching final digests, or an archive-equivalent locator satisfying
10083: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}; if neither field is
10084: present, all bundle-backed support claims remain local/path-verified only.  No
10085: finite record in one coordinate supplies either of the other two coordinates.
10086: \end{proposition}
10087: 
10088: \begin{proof}
10089: The theorem-obligation coordinate is a finite byte/log/digest field.  By
10090: Proposition~\ref{prop:post-inventory-freshness-obstruction}, any inventory write
10091: changes the source state for that field and invalidates pre-inventory or
10092: stale-inventory command records as current support.  Conversely,
10093: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation} proves
10094: that, after the two inventory files exist, rerunning the extractor and retained
10095: finite-record verifiers and then regenerating the digest manifest produces
10096: exactly the finite local support tuple for the first coordinate of
10097: Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form}.  This proves the
10098: first clause and also shows that no weaker pre-inventory row closes the current
10099: theorem-obligation ledger.
10100: 
10101: The venue/bibliography field reads live external route and locator facts, not
10102: the manuscript's theorem inventory.  Theorem~\ref{thm:venue-rule-freshness-non-escalation}
10103: and Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-time-venue-compliance-record-requirement} require a
10104: dated record for any current route-readiness assertion and prevent that record
10105: from becoming venue acceptance or an upload receipt.  Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
10106: is the command-result interface for such a row: the command, source state,
10107: environment, exit code, and log path are the finite fields that make the row
10108: auditable.  Hence, when such a row is retained, the manuscript may use it only
10109: as dated local-readiness evidence; without such a row the only honest readings
10110: are conditional or historical.
10111: 
10112: The external support field is separate again.  Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}
10113: classifies the finite records that instantiate external support: either the
10114: selected support surface is actually uploaded with matching final digests, or a
10115: public archive-equivalent locator supplies the required byte-equality fields.
10116: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-no-receipt-support-surface-non-escalation} and
10117: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-support-coordinate-dichotomy} rule out the
10118: intermediate reading in which local digest rows, support-boundary text, or a
10119: candidate branch path are treated as a completed upload or archive replacement.
10120: 
10121: The three fields have different finite domains: inventory and verifier bytes,
10122: dated venue/bibliography locator metadata, and upload-or-archive byte-equality
10123: metadata.  By Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, a
10124: stronger submitted-support reading that omits any one of these finite fields has
10125: a missing bad subrecord.  Therefore a post-inventory digest tuple proves no
10126: venue compliance or external upload, a dated venue/bibliography row proves no
10127: theorem-ledger fixed point or upload, and an upload-or-archive locator proves no
10128: post-inventory rerun or live venue check unless the corresponding rows are also
10129: present.  This is the claimed exact normal form.
10130: \end{proof}
10131: 
10132: \begin{corollary}[Local fixed point and external-support boundary]
10133: \label{cor:local-fixed-point-external-boundary}
10134: Assume that the current source state contains
10135: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md}, that the
10136: theorem-environment extractor and the three retained finite-record verifiers are
10137: rerun after those inventory files exist, and that
10138: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is regenerated after the resulting
10139: logs are written.  Then the support tuple of
10140: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation} is a
10141: closed local fixed point for the theorem-inventory coordinate of the present
10142: package.  This fixed point does not instantiate external support unless one of
10143: the two external coordinates of
10144: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} is also supplied:
10145: either exact supplemental upload with matching final digests, or an
10146: archive-equivalent public locator with matching file list, path semantics,
10147: manifest identity, SHA--256 rows, and pinned-commit or same-state branch-head
10148: byte equality.  In the absence of such an upload receipt, DOI, completed
10149: supplemental upload, or byte-equality locator, all bundle-backed and
10150: theorem-inventory-backed claims remain local/path-verified only.
10151: \end{corollary}
10152: 
10153: \begin{proof}
10154: The first three hypotheses are exactly the ordered construction required in
10155: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}: the inventory files are
10156: present before the extractor and finite-record verifiers run, their logs are
10157: therefore post-inventory command records, and the final digest table is computed
10158: only after those log bytes exist.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}
10159: then identifies the resulting tuple of inventory files, extractor log, verifier
10160: logs, and digest manifest as the current finite support coordinate.  The replay
10161: criterion of Corollary~\ref{cor:stable-replay-post-inventory-tuple} shows that
10162: later uses of this coordinate factor through the same byte tuple unless a new
10163: inventory write or a new command-result coordinate is added.  Hence the tuple is
10164: closed as a local fixed point for the theorem-inventory coordinate.
10165: 
10166: The remaining assertion is the external-support separation.  The local tuple
10167: contains byte paths, zero-exit command logs, and SHA--256 rows, but none of the
10168: finite fields that Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}
10169: requires for external instantiation.  Exact upload requires the selected
10170: supplemental surface to be supplied to the venue with matching final digests.
10171: Archive replacement requires the public locator test of
10172: Theorem~\ref{thm:public-branch-archive-equivalent-realization}: same finite file
10173: list, same relative paths, same manifest identity, same digest rows, and an
10174: immutable or same-state branch-head byte-equality record.  By
10175: Proposition~\ref{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}, those
10176: external coordinates cannot be inferred from the local bundle, bibliography row,
10177: or digest table alone.  Therefore, unless one of the two external coordinates is
10178: actually supplied, the closed fixed point remains local/path-verified and proves
10179: no upload, DOI, archive replacement, venue acceptance, Lean/BEDC rebuild, daemon
10180: rerun, or dynamic artifact-validation claim.
10181: \end{proof}
10182: 
10183: \begin{proposition}[Current absence of external support-surface instantiation]
10184: \label{prop:current-absence-external-support-surface-instantiation}
10185: For the present source package, the local post-inventory support coordinate of
10186: Corollary~\ref{cor:local-fixed-point-external-boundary} and the digest manifest
10187: \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} do not instantiate the external
10188: support coordinate of
10189: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.  Hence every claim
10190: whose support uses \path{review_bundle/} has only local/path-verified status in
10191: this package unless a further finite record supplies one of the following two
10192: positive coordinates:
10193: \begin{enumerate}
10194: \item an exact supplemental-upload receipt for the selected support surface with
10195: matching final SHA--256 rows; or
10196: \item a public archive-equivalent locator with the same file list, relative-path
10197: semantics, manifest identity, digest rows, and pinned-commit or same-state
10198: branch-head byte-equality record.
10199: \end{enumerate}
10200: In particular, a local digest row, support-boundary text, a candidate branch/path
10201: locator, or a zero-exit local verifier log is never by itself an upload receipt,
10202: DOI, completed supplemental upload, or archive-equivalent byte-equality locator.
10203: \end{proposition}
10204: 
10205: \begin{proof}
10206: The local coordinate supplied by
10207: Corollary~\ref{cor:local-fixed-point-external-boundary} consists of the theorem
10208: inventory bytes, the post-inventory extractor and finite-record verifier logs,
10209: and the regenerated digest manifest.  Those fields are exactly the fields used
10210: by Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} to close the local
10211: theorem-inventory fixed point: path names, command metadata, exit codes, logs,
10212: and SHA--256 rows.  None of these fields records a venue transaction, upload
10213: receipt, DOI minting event, public archive locator, or public byte-equality
10214: comparison.
10215: 
10216: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} gives the exhaustive
10217: normal form for external support in this paper.  Its first case requires the
10218: selected supplemental surface to be uploaded with matching final digests, which
10219: adds a finite upload coordinate absent from the local fixed point.  Its second
10220: case requires a public locator together with file-list, path-semantics,
10221: manifest-identity, digest-row, and pinned-commit or same-state branch-head
10222: byte-equality fields; these fields are likewise absent from the local fixed
10223: point.  The two cases are disjoint from mere local hashing because two ambient
10224: states can have identical local digest rows and local verifier logs while
10225: differing on whether a venue upload occurred or whether a public archive locator
10226: has been byte-compared.
10227: 
10228: The finite-coordinate determination boundary,
10229: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary}, therefore prevents
10230: any verifier or reader using only the local coordinate from inferring either
10231: external coordinate.  Proposition~\ref{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}
10232: and Theorem~\ref{thm:submission-support-fixed-point-normal-form} already classify
10233: the same missing fields as bad subrecords for stronger support readings.  Thus,
10234: until one of the two positive external coordinates is actually supplied, every
10235: \path{review_bundle/}-backed assertion remains local/path-verified and no listed
10236: local record can be read as an upload receipt, DOI, completed supplemental upload,
10237: or archive-equivalent byte-equality locator.
10238: \end{proof}
10239: 
10240: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record four-coordinate submission-support skeleton]
10241: \label{thm:four-coordinate-submission-support-skeleton}
10242: For the current presentation-only package, every support reading used by the
10243: publication-safety theorem chain factors through the certificate-calculus spine of
10244: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine} and then through
10245: exactly the following four finite coordinates, with no gluing between them unless
10246: the missing coordinate is explicitly supplied.
10247: \begin{enumerate}
10248: \item The theorem-obligation coordinate is the post-inventory byte tuple of
10249: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}, obtained
10250: only when \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} exist
10251: before the extractor and retained finite-record verifiers are rerun and before
10252: the final digest manifest is regenerated.
10253: \item The external-support coordinate is either exact supplemental upload or the
10254: archive-equivalent public locator of
10255: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}; local digest rows,
10256: local manifests, and bibliography notes alone do not instantiate it.
10257: \item The imported BEDC source coordinate is the bounded pinned-snapshot
10258: interface of Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} and
10259: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}; stronger readings require
10260: the exact added coordinate they assert, such as exact source text plus byte
10261: equality, a public fetch-and-compare record, a Lean/BEDC rebuild record, or a
10262: semantic-validation record.
10263: \item The implementation-soundness coordinate for Lean builds, BEDC audits,
10264: publication-daemon behaviour, and dynamic artifact validators is absent unless a
10265: row records the command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path for
10266: that actual gate program.
10267: \end{enumerate}
10268: Consequently, after the post-inventory refresh is run, the present manuscript may
10269: use the refreshed theorem-inventory tuple as local finite-record support for the
10270: main theorem chain, but it still proves no upload, archive replacement, public
10271: repository reachability, public byte equality, Lean/BEDC rebuild, daemon rerun,
10272: or dynamic artifact-validation claim beyond rows explicitly carrying those
10273: fields.
10274: \end{theorem}
10275: 
10276: \begin{proof}
10277: The finite-record certificate calculus spine,
10278: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine}, reduces every support
10279: reading to projection, obstruction, and positive finite extension.  The four
10280: coordinates in the statement are the four projections that remain after that
10281: normal form is applied to the current presentation-only support surface.
10282: 
10283: The first coordinate is isolated by
10284: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  Its construction is
10285: ordered: the inventory bytes must exist, the syntactic extractor and retained
10286: finite-record verifiers must run on that post-inventory state, and the digest
10287: manifest must be computed after the resulting logs exist.  Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}
10288: identifies the resulting tuple, and
10289: Corollary~\ref{cor:stable-replay-post-inventory-tuple} shows that later uses of
10290: the tuple are only byte-level replays unless the sequence is rerun from a new
10291: source state.  Thus the theorem-obligation coordinate is available precisely at
10292: the refreshed local finite-record level and not as theorem semantics or as an
10293: external availability claim.
10294: 
10295: The second coordinate is separated by
10296: Theorem~\ref{thm:support-surface-normal-form},
10297: Theorem~\ref{thm:upload-or-archive-instantiation-boundary}, and
10298: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record}.  Those results make
10299: external support a finite field containing either exact venue upload of the
10300: selected support surface or an archive-equivalent locator with matching file
10301: list, path semantics, manifest identity, digest rows, and pinned or same-state
10302: byte equality.  Proposition~\ref{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}
10303: proves that local rows in \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md}, local
10304: manifest fields, and support-boundary records do not contain those upload or locator
10305: fields.  Hence this coordinate cannot be glued out of the refreshed theorem
10306: inventory tuple.
10307: 
10308: The third coordinate is the source-interface boundary proved near the start of
10309: the supplement.  Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness} fixes
10310: the imported records as path, pinned commit, line range, label, extraction date,
10311: and bounded summary; Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality} makes
10312: that finite interface maximal for the present package; and
10313: Theorem~\ref{thm:public-source-reachability-non-escalation} rules out upgrading
10314: it to a current public-source reachability or public byte-equality claim without
10315: a new finite fetch-and-compare record.  Therefore a local bundled snapshot and a
10316: source-interface verifier log support only the bounded snapshot reading stated in
10317: the theorem.
10318: 
10319: The fourth coordinate is exactly the implementation-soundness boundary.
10320: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} requires command, source
10321: state, environment, exit code, and log path for command-result claims;
10322: Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion} and
10323: Corollary~\ref{cor:submitted-gate-implementation-contract-dichotomy} state that
10324: actual gate implementation soundness is obtained only by adding such rows for the
10325: gate programs; and Theorem~\ref{thm:external-interface-projection-no-free-upgrade}
10326: prevents external finite records from implying that stronger interface.  The
10327: command-result closure theorem,
10328: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure}, applies only to rows actually
10329: rerun and recorded; it explicitly excludes Lean/BEDC rebuild success, daemon
10330: execution, theorem-proof semantics, and dynamic artifact validation when those
10331: rows are absent.
10332: 
10333: The four coordinates are independent finite fields of the package state.  If a
10334: proposed support reading adds upload, archive replacement, public-source
10335: reachability, public byte equality, source-theory rebuild success, daemon
10336: execution, or artifact-semantic validation, then it changes at least one of the
10337: four fields just listed.  By
10338: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} the missing field is a
10339: finite bad subrecord, so the stronger reading is rejected.  Conversely, the
10340: refreshed post-inventory tuple supplies exactly the first field, and the other
10341: three fields remain at their conditional or absent levels as stated.  This is the
10342: claimed skeleton and connects the closing support boundary back to the finite
10343: publication-safety chain of Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and
10344: Theorem~\ref{thm:final-local-support-state-reconciliation}.
10345: \end{proof}
10346: 
10347: \begin{theorem}[Finite-record current-package no-free-upgrade closure]
10348: \label{thm:current-package-no-free-upgrade-closure}
10349: In the present source state, the finite-record theorem chain has exactly the
10350: following terminal form.  The local finite-record certificate level is available
10351: only for the rows named by the submitted support tuple of
10352: Theorem~\ref{thm:four-coordinate-submission-support-skeleton}, after the
10353: post-inventory rerun and digest-regeneration order of
10354: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point}.  The central output of
10355: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} is therefore conditional
10356: finite-record software-audit safety under record-gate soundness, not a proof of
10357: the record-gate implementations or of the artifacts and source theories behind
10358: their rows.  None of the five stronger readings below follows from that tuple.
10359: \begin{enumerate}
10360: \item External support for a bundle-backed claim requires either an exact
10361: supplemental-upload receipt for the selected final support surface with matching
10362: digest rows, or an archive-equivalent public locator with matching file list,
10363: path semantics, manifest identity, digest rows, and pinned or same-state
10364: byte-equality evidence.
10365: \item Implementation soundness for an actual gate program requires, for that
10366: program and not merely for the abstract record gate, source path, specification,
10367: source digest or commit, invocation command, runtime environment, input records,
10368: output records, exit code, log path, digest binding to the support surface, and
10369: an assigned implementation-gate pass row.
10370: \item A fresh Lean/BEDC rebuild or axiom-purity claim for this submission state
10371: requires the corresponding formal-source command row: source commit, source
10372: paths, environment, command, exit code, log path, and the axiom-purity or marker
10373: predicate checked by that command.  The bounded source-interface and Lean-marker
10374: rows already present remain path-verified snapshot evidence only.
10375: \item Dynamic artifact-validation semantics for finite witness rows requires a
10376: fresh artifact-validation gate row naming the artifacts, validator source,
10377: validator specification, source state, environment, command, exit code, and log
10378: path.  Finite manifest rows and the Rule~110 status snapshot remain finite
10379: witness records rather than semantic artifact-validity records.
10380: \item Treating the supplement-level route normal forms
10381: Theorems~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness} and
10382: \ref{thm:presentation-route-stronger-reading-classifier} as primary-paper claims
10383: requires no new evidence coordinate, but it exceeds the intended compact
10384: presentation role.  In the CICM presentation route they are admissible only as
10385: supplemental classifiers used to justify the short finite-record safety summary;
10386: they are not additional end-to-end correctness claims and are not needed as
10387: standalone primary-note conclusions.
10388: \end{enumerate}
10389: Moreover these five failures have a common minimal-obstruction normal form.  For
10390: each stronger reading $d$ of an accepted current-package claim, there is a finite
10391: coordinate $m(d)$ selected from: an external upload/archive locator; a
10392: gate-implementation coordinate; a formal-source rebuild or axiom-audit command
10393: row; an artifact-validation command row; or a presentation-placement coordinate.
10394: The reading $d$ is accepted exactly when $m(d)$ is present with the pass records
10395: specified in the corresponding item above.  If $m(d)$ is absent, the pair formed
10396: by the finite stronger-reading tag for $d$ and the missing coordinate $m(d)$ is an
10397: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord: deleting the tag returns to the already accepted
10398: finite-record reading, and filling $m(d)$ removes this obstruction while leaving
10399: all other current-package checks unchanged.
10400: Consequently any statement in the manuscript that appears to assert one of these
10401: five readings is interpreted, for this submission state, as the corresponding
10402: conditional upgrade statement above.  If the required coordinate is absent, the
10403: statement projects back to the finite-record/path-verified reading and has an
10404: inclusion-minimal bad subrecord for the stronger claim.
10405: \end{theorem}
10406: 
10407: \begin{proof}
10408: Theorem~\ref{thm:four-coordinate-submission-support-skeleton} shows that every
10409: support reading factors through four independent finite coordinates: the local
10410: theorem-obligation tuple, the external-support coordinate, the bounded
10411: source-interface coordinate, and the implementation or dynamic-validation
10412: coordinate.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} and
10413: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-record-certificate-calculus-spine} then imply that a
10414: stronger reading is accepted only by adding the finite fields that distinguish it
10415: from the current projection; otherwise two support states can agree on all
10416: currently read fields and disagree on the stronger fact.
10417: 
10418: For item~1, Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record} gives the
10419: two and only two positive external-support normal forms.  Proposition~\ref{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}
10420: and Proposition~\ref{prop:current-absence-external-support-surface-instantiation}
10421: show that local digest rows, local manifests, and bibliography notes omit both an
10422: upload receipt and an archive-equivalent byte-equality locator.  Hence the
10423: listed external field is the missing bad subrecord for any unconditional external
10424: support claim.
10425: 
10426: For item~2, Theorem~\ref{thm:record-gate-implementation-overclaim-obstruction},
10427: Theorem~\ref{thm:concrete-finite-script-implementation-soundness},
10428: Theorem~\ref{thm:six-coordinate-gate-implementation-soundness-skeleton}, and
10429: Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion} separate the
10430: abstract record-gate hypothesis from correctness of the programs that generate
10431: or check rows.  Their proofs identify the necessary implementation coordinate:
10432: program identity, specification, source state, command execution data,
10433: input--output trace, digest binding, and assigned pass row.  Those fields are not
10434: read by the current certificate projection unless a row explicitly carries them,
10435: so implementation soundness cannot be inferred from certificate acceptance.
10436: 
10437: For item~3, Lemma~\ref{lem:source-interface-extraction-correctness},
10438: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality},
10439: Proposition~\ref{prop:formal-source-gate-instantiation}, and
10440: Theorem~\ref{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction} identify
10441: the imported BEDC evidence as bounded path, pinned-snapshot, marker-existence,
10442: and source-interface evidence.  A fresh rebuild or axiom-purity statement is a
10443: command-result statement, so Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
10444: and Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure} require the command,
10445: environment, source state, exit code, and log path for that formal-source gate.
10446: Without those rows, the stronger formal-source reading is not determined by the
10447: current projection.
10448: 
10449: For item~4, Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-witness-manifest-record-interface},
10450: Proposition~\ref{prop:artifact-validation-gate-unique-semantic-upgrade}, and
10451: Corollary~\ref{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary} make finite witness rows
10452: a manifest-level interface and make semantic artifact validity the unique
10453: positive upgrade supplied by an artifact-validation gate.  The Rule~110 status
10454: snapshot and finite manifests therefore witness only the recorded finite rows
10455: unless the fresh validator coordinate named in the statement is supplied.
10456: 
10457: For item~5, Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-five-interface-sharpness} and
10458: Theorem~\ref{thm:presentation-route-stronger-reading-classifier} are already
10459: finite-record projection theorems: they classify how stronger readings fail or
10460: which finite budgets would make them accepted.  Reprinting that classifier as the
10461: central contribution of the primary note would add no certificate coordinate and
10462: would only duplicate the supplement-level normal form.  Hence the compressed
10463: presentation use is exactly the finite-record safety sentence backed by
10464: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface} and the support-boundary summary;
10465: all route-normal-form detail remains available in the supplement.
10466: 
10467: The five cases exhaust the issue coordinates because the first four are the
10468: independent fields in Theorem~\ref{thm:four-coordinate-submission-support-skeleton}
10469: or its formal-source subfield, while the fifth is a presentation-placement
10470: normal form for those same fields.  The displayed minimal-obstruction normal form
10471: is then obtained by applying the finite bad-record argument separately in the
10472: five coordinates.  In item~1, removing the external-support tag leaves a local
10473: path-verified digest claim; adding the upload receipt or archive-equivalent
10474: locator removes exactly that obstruction.  In item~2, removing the
10475: implementation-soundness tag leaves the conditional record-gate reading of
10476: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}; adding the concrete program
10477: coordinate is precisely the upgrade criterion.  In item~3, removing the
10478: formal-source semantic tag leaves the bounded source-interface certificate of
10479: Theorem~\ref{thm:bounded-source-interface-maximality}; adding the rebuild or
10480: axiom-audit command row is the missing finite field.  In item~4, removing the
10481: artifact-semantic tag leaves the manifest/evaluator-record theorem; adding the
10482: artifact-validation gate supplies the unique semantic upgrade.  In item~5,
10483: removing the primary-placement tag leaves the supplement-level classifier cited
10484: as support for the compact finite-record lesson; changing presentation placement
10485: is the only missing coordinate.  Each pair is inclusion-minimal because
10486: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} reads no smaller
10487: coordinate capable of distinguishing the stronger support state from the current
10488: projection.  The final sentence follows by applying the
10489: minimal-bad-subrecord clause of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-certificate-obstruction-skeleton}
10490: to the first missing field in the proposed stronger reading; in the fifth case no
10491: missing evidence field is asserted, so the normal-form result is simply retained
10492: as supplement-level support rather than elevated into an additional primary-paper
10493: claim.
10494: \end{proof}
10495: 
10496: \begin{corollary}[Stage-A2 support-coordinate resolution normal form]
10497: \label{cor:stage-a2-support-coordinate-resolution-normal-form}
10498: For the presentation-only route of this source package, the four issue
10499: coordinates resolved by the terminal theorem chain have the following exact
10500: normal form.
10501: \begin{enumerate}
10502: \item The theorem-inventory support coordinate is present exactly when
10503: \path{theorem_inventory.json} and \path{theorem_inventory.md} exist before the
10504: theorem-environment extractor and retained finite-record verifiers are rerun,
10505: and \path{review_bundle/FINAL_DIGESTS_SHA256.md} is regenerated after those log
10506: bytes exist.  Its content is syntactic and finite-record only.
10507: \item The external support-surface coordinate is absent from the local package
10508: unless an exact supplemental upload or an archive-equivalent public locator with
10509: matching byte-equality fields is added.  Local digest rows and local verifier
10510: logs therefore support only local/path-verified bundle claims.
10511: \item Implementation soundness of actual gate programs is not discharged by the
10512: main theorem.  It remains an explicit record-gate hypothesis unless the package
10513: adds the per-gate implementation coordinate listed in
10514: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-no-free-upgrade-closure}.
10515: \item Dynamic artifact semantics and fresh formal-source semantics are not
10516: current evidence in this route.  Rule~110/GroundCompiler rows, daemon rows,
10517: Lean/BEDC marker rows, axiom-purity rows, and artifact rows remain bounded
10518: finite records unless fresh command-result or evaluator coordinates are supplied.
10519: \end{enumerate}
10520: Thus the current theorem chain is strong enough for the stated scope exactly as
10521: a schema-relative finite-record safety theorem under record-gate soundness, and
10522: any stronger end-to-end reading has a missing finite coordinate rather than an
10523: implicit proof.
10524: \end{corollary}
10525: 
10526: \begin{proof}
10527: Item~1 is precisely the positive local coordinate constructed by
10528: Theorem~\ref{thm:post-inventory-package-fixed-point} and instantiated by
10529: Corollary~\ref{cor:current-post-inventory-support-tuple-instantiation}: the
10530: inventory bytes precede the extractor and verifier logs, and the digest manifest
10531: is computed only after those logs exist.  The extractor boundary results,
10532: Theorem~\ref{thm:deterministic-theorem-environment-extractor-boundary},
10533: Theorem~\ref{thm:extractor-domain-exactness-non-gluing}, and
10534: Theorem~\ref{thm:theorem-inventory-proofchecker-obstruction}, identify the
10535: coordinate as syntactic coverage rather than proof semantics.
10536: 
10537: Item~2 follows from
10538: Corollary~\ref{cor:upload-or-archive-instantiation-record},
10539: Proposition~\ref{prop:no-external-instantiation-from-local-bundle-records}, and
10540: Proposition~\ref{prop:current-absence-external-support-surface-instantiation}.
10541: Those results classify the only positive external-support records and prove that
10542: the local digest and verifier surface does not contain them.
10543: 
10544: Item~3 is the gate-implementation case of
10545: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-gate-implementation-overclaim-obstruction},
10546: Theorem~\ref{thm:implementation-soundness-extension-criterion}, and
10547: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-no-free-upgrade-closure}: the abstract
10548: record-gate soundness premise is a hypothesis read by the certificate calculus,
10549: whereas correctness of a concrete gate implementation requires its own finite
10550: source, specification, command, input--output, log, digest-binding, and pass-row
10551: coordinate.
10552: 
10553: Item~4 combines the formal-source and artifact cases of
10554: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-command-result-closure},
10555: Theorem~\ref{thm:source-interface-semantic-completeness-obstruction},
10556: Proposition~\ref{prop:artifact-validation-gate-unique-semantic-upgrade}, and
10557: Corollary~\ref{cor:artifact-semantic-validity-boundary}.  These results separate
10558: bounded marker, snapshot, manifest, and status rows from fresh rebuild,
10559: axiom-purity, daemon-execution, and evaluator-semantic claims.
10560: 
10561: The final sentence is then an immediate application of
10562: Theorem~\ref{thm:four-coordinate-submission-support-skeleton} and
10563: Theorem~\ref{thm:current-package-no-free-upgrade-closure}: every stronger reading
10564: changes one of the four finite coordinates, and if the corresponding coordinate
10565: is absent the finite-certificate obstruction skeleton supplies the missing bad
10566: subrecord.
10567: \end{proof}
